UnixLib tests (riscos-unixlib)
==============================

Programs built with the new UnixLib, to check /dev/dsp, /dev/midi and
two fixes from the Warzone 2100 port.
Double-click the Obey files. Each first prints the release it came from
(for example "UnixLibTests 5.0.3.1-rc8: ForkOnly"). The programs are Absolute (AIF) files, so the
ELF loader is not needed. The modules for SharedSoundBuffer output
(SharedSound, StreamManager, SharedSoundBuffer) must be loaded.
SharedSound is part of RISC OS; SharedSoundBuffer and StreamManager come
from ssb.zip: download it from Andrew Sellors' RDPClient page,
  https://orac.co.uk/software/rdpclient/rdpclient.html
and merge its !System into yours. John Duffell's own site (now on the
Internet Archive) has more details:
  https://web.archive.org/web/20110920080106/http://www.duffell.riscos.me.uk/
Then see !MIDISynth's LoadSound or OpenTTD's !Run, or run:
  RMEnsure SharedSound 1.07 RMLoad System:Modules.SSound
  RMEnsure StreamManager 0.03 RMLoad System:Modules.StreamMan
  RMEnsure SharedSoundBuffer 0.07 RMLoad System:Modules.SSBuffer

Tone        5 s chord. First line says which output is used.
ToneMono8   8-bit mono 11 kHz        ) formats UnixLib converts for
ToneULaw    mu-law mono 8 kHz        ) SharedSoundBuffer: each should
ToneBE      16-bit big-endian 22 kHz ) sound like the same chord
ToneNB      non-blocking writes, small fragments
ToneDR      the old DigitalRenderer path
Mix         two programs play at once (should hear both)
ExitBug     DigitalRenderer plays; another program quits after 3 s:
            the sound must carry on (the long-standing bug)
ExitBugSSB  the same with SharedSoundBuffer
MIDI        scale and chords through /dev/midi. Needs the MIDISynth
            *module* (not written yet; the !MIDISynth app is not a
            module) or the MIDI module with a MIDI device. Without
            either it says "Operation not supported by device": that's
            the expected result for now.

EABIExit    does ARMEABISupport still know the program when it exits?
            A Wimp task with two threads polls for 3 s, then
            Wimp_CloseDown and exit (as Reel quits). It logs, at each
            stage, the physical page at &8000 (how ARMEABISupport tells
            programs apart) and whether ARMEABISupport finds the program,
            to <Wimp$ScrapDir>.EABIExit, and shows that file: PASS or
            FAIL. A FAIL means its record (and stacks) stay behind, which
            can make a later program trap at start-up ("code 6").
            EABIExitNoClose  without Wimp_CloseDown
            EABIExitBig      with Reel's 40 MB WimpSlot
            EABIExitPlain    a plain command-line program
            EABIExitRunning  exits with its threads still running
            After each, *ARMEABISupport_Info should list no App left by it.

ExitJoin    a thread joined from an atexit() handler: must print PASS,
            no "Aborted" (Warzone 2100 aborted like this on quit)
FsyncRO     fsync() on a read-only file: must print PASS
Ticker      two busy threads in a Wimp task for 20 s while other tasks run
            (have Alarm, a clock or Organizer running). Ends with an error
            box saying PASS: both threads counted more than 0 although the
            task polls far more often than every 2 cs (5.0.3.1; before,
            they got no time and were counted 0). No other program may
            crash. The log
            <Wimp$ScrapDir>.tickertest also shows whether the task handle
            UnixLib saw at start-up matches Wimp_Initialise's.
TickerEarly the same with the threads started before Wimp_Initialise: the
            ticker then stays on while other tasks run (worst case)
TickerStartTask
            as Ticker, and every 5 s it starts a child task (TickerChild,
            which busy-waits 1 s) with Wimp_StartTask
            Run the three Ticker tests twice: first as they are (the
            lines end via=RMA), then after LoadTicker (the PThreadTicker
            module from this folder, 0.03: via=module; an older
            PThreadTicker isn't used, so the lines stay via=RMA). The
            post_switches count is how often the threads were switched
            as Wimp_Poll returned. To keep the module,
            copy PThrTicker into !Boot.Resources.!System.310.Modules
            and RMEnsure it from a !Run file; it can't be killed while a
            program uses it.
            The three Ticker tests set UnixLib$TickerStats, so UnixLib
            appends a line of ticker counters to
            <Wimp$ScrapDir>.TickerStats as each program exits (the child
            adds its own lines). Please send that file and
            <Wimp$ScrapDir>.tickertest after each run (or after all three:
            TickerStats is appended to, tickertest is overwritten).
Sched       sched_get_priority_min/max and pthread_setschedparam:
            must print PASS
LargeFile   files over 2GB (built with -D_FILE_OFFSET_BITS=64): makes a
            3GB file LargeTest in this folder, checks seeking, sizes and
            data past 2GB, deletes it; must print PASS. Needs 3GB free on
            a filing system with files over 2GB, and a few minutes
BigHeap     a heap past 128 MB: allocates up to 320 MB in 16 MB blocks,
            checks them, lists the heap's dynamic areas ("UnixLibTest
            Heap", "UnixLibTest Heap 2"...), then frees them and allocates
            one 200 MB block (bigger than one area). Must print PASS.
            Needs about 350 MB free.
HeapCheck   run after BigHeap has quit: must print PASS (no "UnixLibTest
            Heap" area left behind; the Task Manager should agree)
ForkExec    vfork + an exec that fails, and system(), from a program
            with two busy threads, three times each: the threads must keep
            running. With the PThreadTicker module loaded (LoadTicker), it
            also checks the module still counts the program, and that
            *RMKill PThreadTicker is refused while it runs. Must print
            PASS. Run it once before LoadTicker and once after.
ForkOnly    fork() three times, no threads: must print PASS.
ForkThreads fork() from a program with two busy threads (as ForkExec, with
            fork added): must print PASS. With 5.0.3.1-rc4 this aborted
            ("abort on data transfer") as fork returned in the parent.
HeapProbe   a probe, not a pass/fail test: can one heap block be bigger
            than 128 MB? Tries (A) a second dynamic area placed right
            after the first, and (B) "physical memory pool" areas of
            256 MB, 512 MB and 1 GB with 140 MB mapped in. Removes every
            area it makes. Please send DAProbeLog (written next to it).
            Needs about 300 MB free.

Please report: what each one printed, whether it sounded right, any
clicks or gaps, and the "max queued" figure.
